WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.849
Integer mistakes are easy to underestimate
because numbers look simple.

2
00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:07.115
In real systems, integers often represent
lengths, counts, offsets,

3
00:00:07.195 --> 00:00:10.976
capacities, identifiers, timestamps,
prices, limits, indexes, retry counters,

4
00:00:11.056 --> 00:00:12.028
and protocol fields.

5
00:00:12.108 --> 00:00:13.890
When the meaning of a number is unclear,

6
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:17.211
the program can make the wrong decision
while still compiling cleanly.

7
00:00:17.291 --> 00:00:20.531
Numeric mistakes can affect correctness,
reliability, and security.

8
00:00:20.611 --> 00:00:22.393
A limit may be calculated incorrectly.

9
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:23.229
A count may wrap.

10
00:00:23.309 --> 00:00:25.416
A signed value may be treated as
non-negative.

11
00:00:25.496 --> 00:00:28.034
A large parsed value may drive an
excessive allocation.

12
00:00:28.114 --> 00:00:30.545
A truncated identifier may point to the
wrong record.

13
00:00:30.625 --> 00:00:33.163
A protocol length may be trusted before it
is validated.

14
00:00:33.243 --> 00:00:34.917
Rust gives developers strong tools,

15
00:00:34.997 --> 00:00:37.266
but it does not remove the need for
numeric design.

16
00:00:37.346 --> 00:00:40.478
The language provides explicit integer
types, checked operations,

17
00:00:40.558 --> 00:00:43.529
fallible conversions, safe collections,
and type-driven APIs.

18
00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:47.281
Those tools are most effective when
developers choose types based on meaning and

19
00:00:47.361 --> 00:00:49.791
validate values before they cross trust
boundaries.

20
00:00:49.871 --> 00:00:51.762
Integer safety is also a review practice.

21
00:00:51.842 --> 00:00:54.920
Reviewers should ask what a number
represents, what range is valid,

22
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.701
what unit it uses, whether negative values
are meaningful,

23
00:00:57.781 --> 00:01:01.129
whether conversion can fail, what happens
at minimum and maximum values,

24
00:01:01.209 --> 00:01:04.234
and whether arithmetic behavior has been
selected deliberately.

25
00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:07.770
The defensive goal is to make numeric
assumptions explicit and reviewable.

26
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.307
A Rust codebase should show where numbers
come from, how they are validated,

27
00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:14.087
how they are converted, how arithmetic
failure is handled,

28
00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:17.246
and how numeric values influence memory,
authorization, parsing,

29
00:01:17.326 --> 00:01:19.540
billing, scheduling, or operational
behavior.
